𝗕𝗮𝘀𝗵 𝗧𝗶𝗽: 𝗦𝘁𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗠𝗮𝗻𝗶𝗽𝘂𝗹𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝘄𝗶𝘁𝗵 #, ##, %, 𝗮𝗻𝗱 %%

When working with filenames or paths in shell scripts, you don’t always need sed, awk, or cut. Bash has built-in string operations that make it easier:

🔹 𝗥𝗲𝗺𝗼𝘃𝗲 𝗳𝗿𝗼𝗺 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘁 (#, ##)

${𝘷𝘢𝘳#𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘵𝘦𝘳𝘯} 
Remove the shortest match of pattern from the beginning.

${𝘷𝘢𝘳##𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘵𝘦𝘳𝘯}
Remove the longest match of pattern from the beginning.

Example:
𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘩="/𝘩𝘰𝘮𝘦/𝘶𝘴𝘦𝘳/𝘥𝘰𝘤𝘴/𝘧𝘪𝘭𝘦.𝘵𝘹𝘵"

𝘦𝘤𝘩𝘰 ${𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘩#*/}   
#Output-> home/user/docs/file.txt  (remove up to first /)

𝘦𝘤𝘩𝘰 ${𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘩##*/}  
#Output-> file.txt  (remove up to last /)

🔹 𝗥𝗲𝗺𝗼𝘃𝗲 𝗳𝗿𝗼𝗺 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗲𝗻𝗱 (%, %%)

${𝘷𝘢𝘳%𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘵𝘦𝘳𝘯} 
Remove the shortest match of pattern from the end.

${𝘷𝘢𝘳%%𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘵𝘦𝘳𝘯}
Remove the longest match of pattern from the end.

Example:
𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘩="/𝘩𝘰𝘮𝘦/𝘶𝘴𝘦𝘳/𝘥𝘰𝘤𝘴/𝘧𝘪𝘭𝘦.𝘵𝘹𝘵"

𝘦𝘤𝘩𝘰 ${𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘩%/*}
#Output-> /home/user/docs  (remove last /file.txt)

𝘦𝘤𝘩𝘰 ${𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘩%%/*}  
#Output-> /home  (remove everything after first /)


✅ These little tricks can save you lines of code and make scripts cleaner.

💡 Next time you need to rename files, extract filenames, or trim paths — try #, ##, %, and %%.
